## Board CLI Reference

You have access to `task-board` CLI for managing your work. All writes use the `m` (mutation DSL) subcommand:

```bash
# FIRST/LAST lifecycle commands:
# Use explicit status changes when the task flow requires it.
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-2ya5le, status=analysis)'       # analyst-style work
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-2ya5le, status=development)'    # implementation / testing work
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-2ya5le, status=reviewing)'      # reviewer handoff
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-2ya5le, status=blocked)'        # when blocked
task-board m 'set_status(TASK-260830-2ya5le, status=to-review)'      # when your work is ready for review

# Track progress with checklist
task-board m 'check_item(TASK-260830-2ya5le, item=1)'                        # check item N
task-board m 'add_checklist_item(TASK-260830-2ya5le, text="Write tests")'    # add checklist item

# Add notes about your progress, decisions, blockers, or review findings
task-board m 'set_notes(TASK-260830-2ya5le, text="your note here")'

# Save short text directly as outcome resources
task-board m 'add_resource(TASK-260830-2ya5le, name=TASK-260830-2ya5le_results.md, content="...", type=outcome, description="Description")'

# Attach an existing local file (screenshots, PDFs, logs, archives, research docs)
task-board resource add TASK-260830-2ya5le ./path/to/file --type outcome --name TASK-260830-2ya5le_artifact.bin -d "Description"
```

## Spawn Run Control

Tracked runs expose `TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID`. Check directives at safe checkpoints,
at most once per 1 minute, never in a loop. Directives have no delivery timer.

```bash
task-board spawn status "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
task-board spawn directives "$TASK_BOARD_RUN_ID"
```

No inbound push: directives are cooperative checkpoint signals:
- persist your current notes/artifacts before acting on `cancel`-style requests
